Here's a complete implementation for a C# MCP server that wraps a REST API:

---

**Program.cs** (stdio transport):

```csharp
using Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection;
using Microsoft.Extensions.Hosting;
using Microsoft.Extensions.Logging;

var builder = Host.CreateApplicationBuilder(args);

// Logging must go to stderr — stdout is the JSON-RPC protocol channel
builder.Logging.AddConsole(options =>
    options.LogToStandardErrorThreshold = LogLevel.Trace);

builder.Services.AddHttpClient("WeatherApi", client =>
{
    client.BaseAddress = new Uri("https://api.weather.example.com");
});

builder.Services.AddMcpServer()
    .WithStdioServerTransport()
    .WithToolsFromAssembly();

await builder.Build().RunAsync();
```

**WeatherTools.cs** (tool class wrapping a REST API):

```csharp
using System.ComponentModel;
using System.Text.Json;
using Microsoft.Extensions.Logging;
using ModelContextProtocol.Server;

[McpServerToolType]
public class WeatherTools(IHttpClientFactory httpFactory, ILogger<WeatherTools> logger)
{
    [McpServerTool, Description("Get the current weather forecast for a city.")]
    public async Task<string> GetForecast(
        [Description("City name, e.g. 'Seattle'")] string city,
        [Description("Unit: 'metric' or 'imperial'")] string units = "metric",
        CancellationToken cancellationToken = default)
    {
        logger.LogInformation("Fetching forecast for {City}", city);
        var client = httpFactory.CreateClient("WeatherApi");
        var response = await client.GetAsync(
            $"/v1/forecast?city={Uri.EscapeDataString(city)}&units={units}",
            cancellationToken);
        response.EnsureSuccessStatusCode();
        return await response.Content.ReadAsStringAsync(cancellationToken);
    }

    [McpServerTool(ReadOnly = true), Description("List all supported cities.")]
    public async Task<string> ListCities(CancellationToken cancellationToken = default)
    {
        var client = httpFactory.CreateClient("WeatherApi");
        return await client.GetStringAsync("/v1/cities", cancellationToken);
    }
}
```

---

## How DI Works for MCP Tools

The SDK integrates directly with .NET's standard `IServiceProvider`:

| Pattern | How it works |
|---------|--------------|
| **Constructor injection** (non-static class) | The SDK instantiates your `[McpServerToolType]` class per-request, resolving constructor params from DI. Use primary constructors as shown above. |
| **Method parameter injection** (static class) | DI services can appear as method parameters directly. The SDK distinguishes them from tool inputs—they won't appear in the schema. |
| **Auto-injected types** | `CancellationToken`, `IMcpServer`, `IProgress<ProgressNotificationValue>`, and any DI-registered service are resolved automatically and hidden from the LLM. |

**Key rule:** Only parameters with `[Description]` are exposed in the tool schema. Everything else is injected silently.

To scaffold this project from scratch:

```bash
dotnet new mcpserver -n WeatherMcpServer
cd WeatherMcpServer
dotnet add package Microsoft.Extensions.Http
dotnet build
```
